// PLUTARCH, ON ISIS AND OSIRIS, IN PLAIN WORDS: a close paraphrase of each of
// the 80 chapters in Frank Cole Babbitt's English (Loeb Classical Library,
// 1936). Plutarch writes as a Greek, a priest of Apollo at Delphi, to Clea, a
// priestess there; the book is a Greek philosophical essay about Egyptian
// religion, not an Egyptian text. Three voices are kept apart, as Plutarch
// keeps them:
//   - the myth, which he tells in reported speech ("they relate"): "Plutarch
//     retells the story as the Egyptians tell it ...";
//   - what others say or believe, Egyptian priests, Greek writers (Manetho,
//     Hecataeus, Eudoxus, the Stoics, Pythagoreans, Zoroaster as reported, and
//     so on): "Plutarch reports ...", "X says ...";
//   - his own judgements and reading: "Plutarch, in his own voice, ...".
// Where a chapter mixes them, the paraphrase changes voice where he does.
// Nothing here is offered as true of Egypt or of the gods: it is what
// Babbitt's English says Plutarch says. Babbitt's footnotes are not
// paraphrased.

export const PLAIN_PLUTARCH_ISIS_OSIRIS: Record<string, string> = {
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1–11: the search for truth, the priests' customs
  "greek.plut.1":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, tells Clea that sensible people should ask the gods for every good thing, and above all for knowledge of the gods themselves, as far as humans can have it. Nothing is a greater gift for a man to receive, or for God to give, than truth. God gives men other things they want, but sense and intelligence he shares only in part, because they are his own; his happiness lies not in gold or thunderbolts but in knowledge and understanding. Plutarch quotes Homer, who said that Zeus was the elder born and knew more, and takes it to mean that Zeus's rank is the nobler because he is older in wisdom. Part of the happiness of the gods' eternal life is that nothing escapes their foreknowledge; without knowledge, immortality would be not life but mere passing time.",
  "greek.plut.2":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, says that seeking the truth, above all about the gods, is a longing for the divine, holier than any ritual purity or temple service, and pleasing to Isis, the goddess Clea serves, who is wise and loves wisdom. He explains her name as Greek, from knowing, and her enemy Typhon's as Greek too, from conceit, since Typhon is puffed up by ignorance. Typhon tears apart and scatters the sacred teaching; Isis gathers it, puts it together and hands it to those initiated into her rites. Their discipline of diet and chastity curbs the love of pleasure and trains them for the hard services of the shrines, whose aim is to know the First, the Lord of All, whom the goddess urges us to seek because he is with her. Even her shrine's name, Iseion, promises understanding of what truly is, to those who enter with reason and reverence.",
  "greek.plut.3":
    "Plutarch reports that many writers make Isis the daughter of Hermes, and many others the daughter of Prometheus, because Prometheus discovered wisdom and forethought and Hermes grammar and music; so, he says, at Hermopolis the first of the Muses is called both Isis and Justice. In his own voice he says that Isis reveals divine things to those truly called 'bearers of the sacred vessels' and 'wearers of the sacred robes': people who carry the sacred teaching about the gods within their souls, free of superstition, and hint at it, darkly or clearly, as their robes do. That is why her dead votaries are dressed in those robes. But, he tells Clea, a beard and a rough cloak do not make a philosopher, and linen and a shaved head do not make a votary of Isis: the true votary is the one who, having received the rites in due form, uses reason to search out the truth in them.",
  "greek.plut.4":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, takes up a small matter most people do not understand: why the priests shave their hair and wear linen. He reports the explanations others give, that they revere the sheep and so avoid its wool and flesh, that they shave in mourning, and that they wear linen because the flax flower is the colour of the sky, and rejects them. The one true reason, he says, is in Plato's words, that it is against divine law for the impure to touch the pure: hair, wool, fur and nails grow from waste matter, so it would be absurd to shave one's own body smooth and then wear an animal's hair. He reads a line of Hesiod, about not paring the nails at a feast of the gods, the same way. Flax, by contrast, springs from the immortal earth, gives food, and makes clean, light clothing that suits every season and is least apt to breed lice.",
  "greek.plut.5":
    "Plutarch reports that the priests so dislike whatever leaves a residue that they avoid most beans, mutton and pork, and take no salt in their times of purity, chiefly because salt sharpens the appetite for food and drink. He calls 'silly' the view of Aristagoras that salt is impure because tiny creatures die in it as it forms. He reports too that the Apis bull is watered from its own well and kept from the Nile, not because the river is thought unclean (nothing is more honoured in Egypt) but because Nile water is said to fatten; the priests want the bodies of the Apis and of themselves to be light, so that the mortal part does not weigh down the divine.",
  "greek.plut.6":
    "Plutarch reports the priests' rules about wine. At Heliopolis none is brought into the shrine, because it is not fitting to drink by day under the eyes of their Lord the Sun; elsewhere it is used, but sparingly, and forbidden in their seasons of purity, which they spend studying and teaching divine things. The kings, who were priests, drank a measured amount prescribed by the sacred writings, as Hecataeus records. Drinking began, he reports, only under Psammetichus; before that the Egyptians would not drink wine or pour it to the gods, thinking it the blood of those who once fought against the gods, from whose fallen bodies, mingled with the earth, the vine sprang; that is why drunkenness drives men mad, filling them with the blood of their forbears. Eudoxus, he adds, says the priests tell these tales.",
  "greek.plut.7":
    "Plutarch reports the Egyptian rules about fish. Not all Egyptians avoid all sea-fish: the people of Oxyrhynchus avoid fish caught with a hook, for fear the hook has caught the oxyrhynchus fish they revere, and the people of Syene avoid the phagrus, which is said to appear when the Nile rises and so to herald the flood. The priests avoid all fish, and on the ninth day of the first month, when other Egyptians eat broiled fish before their doors, they burn theirs instead. Of their two reasons he keeps the religious one for later, since it belongs to the story of Osiris and Typhon; the plain one is that fish is a needless luxury, as Homer shows, whose Phaeacians, Ithacans and even Odysseus's men at sea eat none until in dire want. They also hold the sea to be a foul residue outside the order of the world, not one of its elements.",
  "greek.plut.8":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, says that nothing irrational, fabulous or superstitious has a place in the Egyptian rites: some have a moral or practical value, others a basis in history or natural science. He reports examples. The priests shun the onion, not because of the incredible tale that Dictys, Isis's foster-child, drowned reaching for onions, but because the onion alone thrives as the moon wanes and makes men thirsty at fasts and tearful at feasts. They count the pig unclean because it mates as the moon wanes and its milk was thought to cause skin disease; the story told at their one yearly sacrifice of a pig, at full moon, that Typhon found Osiris's coffin while hunting a boar and tore the body apart, not all of them accept. He reports too that a pillar at Thebes bore curses on King Meinis for leading the Egyptians away from simple living, set up by Technactis, father of Bocchoris, who came to love plain food and a straw bed on campaign.",
  "greek.plut.9":
    "Plutarch reports that the kings were chosen from the priests or the warrior class, and that a warrior made king at once became a priest and shared in their philosophy, which is mostly veiled in myths and in words that give only glimpses of the truth; the sphinxes before the shrines, he says, show that their teaching is a riddling wisdom. He reports that at Saïs the seated statue of Athena, whom they take to be Isis, bore the inscription 'I am all that has been, and is, and shall be, and my robe no mortal has yet uncovered.' He reports three views of the name Amoun: most people take it for the Egyptian name of Zeus, which the Greeks call Ammon; Manetho of Sebennytus thinks it means 'concealed' or 'concealment'; Hecataeus of Abdera says it is a word used to call someone. On that view the Egyptians call on the supreme god, whom they identify with the universe, as one hidden and unseen, asking him to show himself. So careful, he says, were the Egyptians in their wisdom about the gods.",
  "greek.plut.10":
    "Plutarch reports that the wisest Greeks, Solon, Thales, Plato, Eudoxus and Pythagoras (and some add Lycurgus), went to Egypt and studied with the priests: Eudoxus with Chonuphis of Memphis, Solon with Sonchis of Saïs, Pythagoras with Oenuphis of Heliopolis. Pythagoras, it seems, admired the priests, copied their symbols and secret teachings, and put his doctrines into riddles; Plutarch, in his own voice, compares Pythagorean precepts such as 'Do not eat upon a stool' and 'Do not poke a fire with a sword' to hieroglyphs, and the Pythagoreans' naming of numbers after gods to the images in Egyptian shrines. He reports Egyptian emblems: Osiris drawn as an eye and a sceptre (some explaining the name as 'many-eyed', from os, 'many', and iri, 'eye'); the heavens as a heart over a censer; at Thebes, statues of judges without hands and a chief judge with eyes shut, for justice that cannot be bribed or swayed; and the warriors' seals carved with a beetle, because, as they believed, all beetles are male.",
  "greek.plut.11":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, tells Clea that whenever she hears the Egyptians' tales of the gods' wanderings, dismemberments and sufferings, she must not think they happened as told. The dog is not called Hermes as its name: its watchfulness and its knowing friend from foe are likened to the cleverest god. The sun is not believed to rise as a new-born child from a lotus: the image shows the sun kindled from the waters. The cruel Persian king Ochus, who killed and ate the Apis, they call 'the Sword', likening his nature to a weapon, not naming his being. If she hears the stories in this way, from those who interpret them reverently and philosophically, keeps the established rites, and believes that no sacrifice or deed pleases the gods more than a true belief about them, she will avoid superstition, which is no less an evil than atheism.",
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 12–19: the myth, as "they relate" it
  "greek.plut.12":
    "Plutarch retells the story, as the Egyptians tell it, in the briefest form. The Sun, learning that Rhea had lain with Cronus, cursed her so that she could give birth in no month and no year. But Hermes, who loved her, lay with her too, and then, playing at draughts with the Moon, won a seventieth of each of her periods of light and made five extra days, added to the 360. The Egyptians still call them the added days and keep them as the gods' birthdays. On the first Osiris was born, and a voice cried, 'The Lord of All advances to the light'; some say a certain Pamyles of Thebes heard a voice from the shrine of Zeus bidding him proclaim the birth of a great and good king, Osiris, and was given the child to rear, whence the festival of the Pamylia. On the second day Arueris, whom the Greeks call Apollo and some the elder Horus, was born; on the third Typhon, who broke out through his mother's side; on the fourth Isis, in the ever-moist regions; on the fifth Nephthys. Some say Osiris and Arueris were the Sun's children, Isis Hermes's, and Typhon and Nephthys Cronus's; so the kings counted the third day unlucky. Nephthys married Typhon; Isis and Osiris loved each other in the womb before they were born, and some say Arueris came of that union.",
  "greek.plut.13":
    "Plutarch retells the story as they relate it. Reigning in Egypt, Osiris freed the Egyptians from a poor and brutish life, showing them crops, giving them laws and teaching them to honour the gods; then he travelled the whole earth, civilizing it without arms, by persuasion, song and music, so that the Greeks took him for Dionysus. While he was away Isis kept watch and Typhon tried nothing; on his return Typhon plotted with seventy-two conspirators and an Ethiopian queen named Aso. He secretly measured Osiris, had a beautiful chest made to his size, brought it to a feast and promised it to whoever fitted it. All tried; when Osiris lay down in it the plotters slammed the lid, nailed it, sealed it with molten lead, and sent it down the river to the sea through the Tanitic mouth, which the Egyptians still call hateful. They date the deed to the seventeenth of Athyr, when the sun is in Scorpion, in the twenty-eighth year of Osiris's reign, or, some say, of his life.",
  "greek.plut.14":
    "Plutarch retells the story as they relate it. The Pans and Satyrs near Chemmis were first to learn of the deed and spread it, which is why a crowd's sudden fright is still called a 'panic'. Isis cut off a lock of her hair and put on mourning where the city of Kopto still bears the name (some take the name to mean 'deprivation'). She wandered everywhere, questioning everyone, even small children, who had in fact seen the chest and told her by which river mouth it had gone out to sea; so, he reports, the Egyptians think children can foretell the future, especially when playing in holy places. Isis also learned that Osiris had lain with her sister Nephthys, mistaking her for Isis, and found the proof in a garland of melilot he had left with her. Nephthys had exposed the child at birth for fear of Typhon; Isis searched for it, found it with the help of dogs, and raised it, and it became her guard, named Anubis, said to guard the gods as dogs guard men.",
  "greek.plut.15":
    "Plutarch retells the story as they relate it. Isis learned that the sea had cast the chest ashore near Byblus, where it lodged in a clump of heather; the heather quickly grew into a great trunk that enclosed and hid it, and the king of the country, admiring it, cut it and used it as a pillar to hold up his roof. Learning this by the divine inspiration of Rumour, Isis came to Byblus and sat by a spring in tears, speaking to no one but the queen's maidservants, whose hair she plaited and whose bodies she made wonderfully fragrant from her own. The queen longed to see the stranger, sent for her, and made her nurse to her baby. The king's name, they say, was Malcander; the queen's is given as Astartê, Saosis, or Nemanûs, which in Greek would be Athenaïs.",
  "greek.plut.16":
    "Plutarch retells the story as they relate it. Isis nursed the baby by giving it her finger to suck instead of her breast, and at night burned away the mortal parts of its body, while she herself, turned into a swallow, flew wailing round the pillar. The queen, who had been watching, saw her child in the fire and cried out, and so robbed it of immortality. Then the goddess revealed herself and asked for the pillar; she took it down with ease, cut away the heather wood round the chest, wrapped the wood in linen, anointed it with perfume and gave it to the king and queen, and the people of Byblus still venerate it in the shrine of Isis. Throwing herself on the coffin, she wailed so terribly that the king's younger son died on the spot. She kept the elder with her, put the coffin on a boat and set sail; when the river Phaedrus raised a rough wind at dawn, she grew angry and dried up its stream.",
  "greek.plut.17":
    "Plutarch retells the story as they relate it. Alone at the first quiet place, Isis opened the chest, laid her face on the dead face, caressed it and wept. The king's son crept up behind her and saw; she turned and gave him one terrible look of anger, and he died of fright. Others say he fell overboard. He too is honoured for the goddess's sake: some say he is the Maneros of whom the Egyptians sing at their feasts and who invented music, others that he was called Palaestinus or Pelusius and that the city she founded is named after him, and others that 'maneros' is no name at all but a drinking cry meaning 'good luck in such things!' In his own voice Plutarch adds that the figure of a corpse carried round in a chest at Egyptian feasts is not a reminder of what happened to Osiris, as some think, but a prompt to enjoy the present, since all will soon be as that figure is.",
  "greek.plut.18":
    "Plutarch retells the story as they relate it. Isis went to her son Horus, who was being reared at Buto, and hid the chest out of the way; but Typhon, hunting by moonlight, came upon it, recognized the body, cut it into fourteen pieces and scattered them. Isis searched for them again, sailing through the marshes in a papyrus boat, which is why, they say, crocodiles do not harm people in such boats. Hence the many so-called tombs of Osiris in Egypt: she held a funeral for each piece where she found it. Others say she had images of him made and gave them to many cities as if they were his body, so that he would be honoured in more places and Typhon, if he beat Horus, could not find the true tomb. The one part she never found was the male member, thrown at once into the river and eaten by the lepidotus, the sea-bream and the pike, the fish the Egyptians most strictly avoid; Isis made a replica of it and consecrated the phallus, still honoured with a festival.",
  "greek.plut.19":
    "Plutarch retells the story as they relate it. Osiris came back to Horus from the other world and trained him for battle. He asked what Horus thought the noblest thing, and Horus said, to avenge one's father and mother; and which animal most useful in war, and Horus said, the horse rather than the lion, since a lion helps a man in need but a horse cuts off and destroys a fleeing enemy. Osiris was pleased and judged him ready. Many went over to Horus, among them Typhon's concubine Thueris; a serpent that pursued her was cut to pieces by Horus's men, which is why, they say, people throw down a rope and chop it up. The battle lasted many days and Horus won. Isis, given Typhon in chains, let him go; Horus in anger seized the crown from her head, and Hermes gave her a cow-headed helmet instead. Typhon then accused Horus of being illegitimate, but with Hermes pleading for him the gods judged him legitimate, and Typhon was beaten in two more battles. Osiris lay with Isis after his death, and she bore Harpocrates, born before his time and weak in his lower limbs.",
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 20–31: how not to read the myth; demigods
  "greek.plut.20":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, says these are nearly all the main points of the story, leaving out the most shameful parts, such as the dismemberment of Horus and the beheading of Isis. If people held such things to have really happened to blessed and imperishable beings, then, as Aeschylus says, one would need to spit and cleanse the mouth; Clea herself, he says, detests those who think so. But these tales are not idle fictions spun by poets like a spider's web: they tell of puzzling events and experiences. As the rainbow is a reflection of the sun on a cloud, the stories are reflections of some true account that turns the mind to other things. He points to the mournful sacrifices, to temples with open courts and dark underground chambers, and to the shrines of Osiris, whose body is said to lie in many places. He reports what is said: that a small town, Diochites, alone has the true tomb; that the great men of Egypt seek burial at Abydos, to lie in the same ground as Osiris; that at Memphis the Apis is kept as the image of Osiris's soul, and his body lies there too; and that the sacred island by Philae is untrodden, even by birds and fish, except at one season, when the priests cross to it, sacrifice for the dead and lay wreaths on the tomb beneath a tall persea tree.",
  "greek.plut.21":
    "Plutarch reports that Eudoxus says Osiris's body lies at Busiris, his birthplace, and that the name Taphosiris itself means 'tomb of Osiris'. He passes over, as touching secret rites, the cutting of wood, the tearing of linen and the libations. He reports the priests' teaching that the bodies of these gods, and of all gods except those without beginning or end, are kept by the priests after their labours, while their souls shine as stars: the soul of Isis is the star the Greeks call the Dog-star and the Egyptians Sothis, that of Horus is Orion, and that of Typhon the Bear. He reports too that all Egyptians pay a set share for burying the sacred animals except the people of the Theban region, who believe in no mortal god but only in Kneph, who had no beginning and will have no end.",
  "greek.plut.22":
    "Plutarch reports a view he will go on to reject: that these stories record the deeds and sufferings of kings and despots who, through great virtue or power, claimed to be called gods and later fell to fortune. That view, he says, is the easiest way out of the story and moves the shame from gods to men, and it has some support in Egyptian tradition, which says that Hermes had thin arms and big elbows, that Typhon was red, Horus white and Osiris dark, as though they were mortal men; that Osiris was a general and Canopus his pilot, after whom the star is named; and that the ship the Greeks call Argo, shaped like Osiris's ship, was set among the stars in his honour, near Orion and the Dog-star, which the Egyptians hold sacred to Horus and Isis.",
  "greek.plut.23":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, hesitates to accept that view, for it would be 'moving the immovable', warring against time and against the piety of countless peoples, bringing the gods' names down from heaven to earth, and destroying the reverence implanted in nearly all mankind at birth. It would throw the doors open to the godless and give free rein to the frauds of Euhemerus of Messene, who, he says, invented an incredible mythology of his own and spread atheism everywhere by turning all the gods into generals, admirals and kings of long ago, supposedly recorded in golden letters at Panchon, which no one but Euhemerus ever saw, on a voyage to peoples who never existed.",
  "greek.plut.24":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, grants that great deeds are told of Semiramis in Assyria and Sesostris in Egypt, that the Phrygians call brilliant exploits 'manic' after their early king Manes (or Masdes), and that Cyrus and Alexander won victory after victory, yet these have only the fame of noble kings. Those who in arrogance, as Plato says, had themselves called gods and given temples flourished but a brief time and then, convicted of vanity and imposture, vanished like smoke, dragged from their altars like runaway slaves, with nothing left but their tombs. He gives two examples: the elder Antigonus, hailed in a poem as 'Offspring of the Sun and a god', replied that the slave who carried out his chamber-pot knew nothing of it; and the sculptor Lysippus rightly found fault with the painter Apelles for giving Alexander a thunderbolt, when he had given him a spear, a glory that was truly his own.",
  "greek.plut.25":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, judges it better to hold that the stories of Typhon, Osiris and Isis tell what happened neither to gods nor to men but to demigods (daemons). He reports that Plato, Pythagoras, Xenocrates and Chrysippus, following early writers on sacred things, say such beings are stronger than men but do not have the divine nature pure: they share in soul and in bodily sense, feel pleasure and pain, and differ among themselves in virtue and vice, as men do. The Greek tales of the Giants and Titans, of Cronus's lawless deeds, of Python's fight with Apollo, of Dionysus's flights and Demeter's wanderings are no less strange than those of Osiris and Typhon, and the things hidden in mystery rites are to be explained in the same way.",
  "greek.plut.26":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, gathers witnesses for demigods. Homer, he says, calls the good 'god-like' but uses 'daemon' of the worthy and worthless alike, as in lines he quotes. Plato gives the Olympian gods right-hand things and odd numbers, and the demigods the opposite. He reports Xenocrates' view that unlucky days and festivals with beatings, lamentation, fasting or obscene talk honour not the gods or good demigods but great, powerful and sullen beings in the air around us that enjoy such things and, if they get them, do nothing worse. Hesiod calls the good demigods 'holy deities', 'guardians of mortals' and 'givers of wealth'; Plato calls them interpreters and servants between gods and men, carrying prayers up and oracles and gifts down; and Empedocles says demigods pay for their sins, chased by sky, sea, earth and sun in turn, each receiving them with loathing, until, purified, they regain their natural place.",
  "greek.plut.27":
    "Plutarch reports that stories like these are told of Typhon: that out of jealousy and enmity he did terrible things, threw everything into confusion, filled earth and sea with evils, and was later punished. Isis, sister and wife of Osiris, his avenger, having quenched Typhon's fury, would not let her struggles, wanderings and many acts of wisdom and courage be forgotten; she wove images and representations of her sufferings into the most holy rites and consecrated them, as a lesson in piety and a comfort for men and women in like troubles. She and Osiris were changed for their virtue from good demigods into gods, as Heracles and Dionysus were later, and so receive the honours of both; their power reaches everywhere but is greatest above and below the earth. Some, he reports, say that Pluto is Serapis and Persephone is Isis, as Archemachus of Euboea says, and Heracleides Ponticus, who holds the oracle at Canopus to be Pluto's.",
  "greek.plut.28":
    "Plutarch reports how the statue of Serapis came to Alexandria. Ptolemy Soter dreamed of the colossal statue of Pluto at Sinope, which he had never seen, and it told him to bring it to Alexandria. A well-travelled man named Sosibius said he had seen such a statue at Sinope, and Ptolemy sent Soteles and Dionysius, who after a long time, with great difficulty and some help from providence, stole it and brought it away. When it was shown in Egypt, Timotheus, the expounder of sacred law, and Manetho of Sebennytus judged from the Cerberus and the serpent with it that it was Pluto, and persuaded Ptolemy that it was no other god than Serapis; it had not borne that name at Sinope, but took in Alexandria the name the Egyptians give Pluto. Plutarch adds that Heracleitus says Hades and Dionysus are the same, and dismisses as too frivolous the allegory that the body is Hades. In his own voice he says it is better to identify Osiris with Dionysus, and Serapis with Osiris, who took that name when he changed his nature; so Serapis is a god for all peoples, as Osiris is, as initiates know.",
  "greek.plut.29":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, sets aside the Phrygian writings that make Serapis a son of Heracles and Isis his daughter, and Phylarchus's claim that Dionysus brought two bulls, Apis and Osiris, from India; he reports Phylarchus's derivation of Serapis from sairein, to sweep or set in order, and calls it absurd. More absurd still, he says, is the view that Serapis is no god but the name of the Apis's coffin, with the bronze Gates of Oblivion and Lamentation at Memphis that sound at the Apis's burial. More moderate is the derivation from words for rushing movement, the motion of the universe. Most of the priests, he reports, say that Osiris and Apis are joined into one, the Apis being the bodily image of Osiris's soul. His own opinion is that, if the name is Egyptian, it means joy, since the Egyptians call their feast of rejoicing sairei; Plato, too, says Hades is named as a kindly god to those who dwell with him. He reports that Amenthes, the place beneath the earth where souls go, means 'the one who receives and gives', and leaves for later whether such words came from Greece long ago and were brought back.",
  "greek.plut.30":
    "Plutarch reports how the Egyptians treat Typhon, now that Osiris and Isis have become gods and his power, crushed but still struggling, remains. They try to soothe him with some sacrifices; at other festivals they humiliate him, jeering at red-haired men and, at Kopto, throwing an ass over a cliff, because Typhon was red-haired and ass-coloured. The people of Busiris and Lycopolis use no trumpets, because they bray like an ass; the ass is held unclean, and in the months Payni and Phaophi cakes are stamped with a bound ass. At the sacrifice to the Sun, worshippers must wear no gold and give no fodder to an ass. He reports too that the Pythagoreans plainly count Typhon a daemonic power, assigning him a figure of fifty-six sides, as Eudoxus records, while the triangle belongs to Hades, Dionysus and Ares, the square to Rhea, Aphrodite, Demeter, Hestia and Hera, and the twelve-sided figure to Zeus.",
  "greek.plut.31":
    "Plutarch reports that, believing Typhon to be red, the Egyptians sacrifice red cattle, so strictly inspected that one black or white hair disqualifies a beast; what they sacrifice is not what the gods love but the opposite, animals in which, they think, the souls of wicked men are housed. So they curse the victim's head and cut it off, once throwing it into the river and now selling it to foreigners. The priests called 'Sealers' marked the victim with a seal showing, as Castor records, a kneeling man with hands bound and a sword at his throat. The ass, he repeats, suffers for its stupidity and lewdness as well as its colour; so they nicknamed the hated Persian king Ochus 'the Ass', and he answered, 'This Ass will feast upon your Bull', and killed the Apis, as Deinon records. He reports, and rejects, the tale that Typhon fled the battle on an ass for seven days and then fathered Hierosolymus and Judaeus: those who tell it, he says, are plainly dragging Jewish traditions into the story.",
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 32–45: the "natural" interpretations
  "greek.plut.32":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, begins again with those who explain the story more philosophically, comparing them to Greeks who say Cronus means Time and Hera Air. He reports that such Egyptians say Osiris is the Nile uniting with the Earth, which is Isis, and Typhon the sea, into which the Nile flows and is lost, except for what the earth takes up and is fertilized by. He reports that a lament sung for Cronus mourns one born on the left and dissolved on the right, because the Egyptians take the east as the face of the world, the north as its right and the south as its left; the Nile, rising in the south and swallowed by the sea in the north, is born on the left and dies on the right. So the priests keep away from the sea, call salt 'Typhon's foam', will not set salt on the table or speak to pilots, and avoid fish; they draw a fish to mean hatred. At Saïs, in the forecourt of Athena's temple, were carved a babe, an old man, a hawk, a fish and a hippopotamus, meaning 'You who come into the world and leave it, God hates shamelessness'. He adds the Pythagorean saying that the sea is a tear of Cronus.",
  "greek.plut.33":
    "Plutarch reports that the wiser priests call Osiris not just the Nile but the whole source and power of moisture, the cause of generation and the substance of seed, and call Typhon everything dry, fiery and parched, all that is hostile to moisture; and because they think Typhon was reddish-sallow, they avoid meeting men of that colour. Osiris, in their tradition, was dark, because water darkens earth, clothes and clouds: moisture makes young people's hair black, while dryness greys it, and moist spring is vigorous and healthy while dry autumn harms plants and animals. The black bull Mneuis at Heliopolis, sacred to Osiris and by some thought the sire of the Apis, is honoured next after the Apis. They call Egypt, with its very black soil, by the name of the black of the eye, 'Chemia', and liken it to a heart, warm and moist and lying on the left side of the inhabited world as the heart does in a man.",
  "greek.plut.34":
    "Plutarch reports that the Egyptians say the sun and moon sail round in boats, not chariots, hinting that they are fed by and born from moisture; and that they think Homer, like Thales, learned from the Egyptians to make water the origin of all things, Oceanus being Osiris and Tethys Isis, the kindly nurse of all. He adds Greek word-play he sets beside this: the words for emission and union, and for son, from water and rain; and the name Hyes given to Dionysus as lord of moisture, who, he says, is no other than Osiris. Hellanicus, he reports, seems to have heard the priests say 'Hysiris', and spells the name so.",
  "greek.plut.35":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, says that no one knows better than Clea that Osiris is the same as Dionysus, since she leads the inspired women of Delphi and was consecrated by her parents in the rites of Osiris. For others he gives public proofs, leaving the secret ones untold. He reports that at the burial of the Apis the priests, wearing fawn-skins and carrying Bacchic wands, shout and move like Dionysus's ecstatic followers. He sets beside it Greek customs: statues of Dionysus as a bull; the women of Elis praying that he come 'with the hoof of a bull'; the Argives calling him 'Son of the Bull' and summoning him from the water with trumpets, hidden in wands, while casting a lamb to the Keeper of the Gate, as Socrates writes. The Greek tales of the Titans and the night rites agree with the dismemberment, revival and rebirth of Osiris, and the tombs agree too: the Egyptians show tombs of Osiris in many places, and the Delphians believe Dionysus's remains lie beside the oracle, where the Holy Ones offer a secret sacrifice when the god's devotees wake him. Pindar's lines show Dionysus as lord of all moisture, and so Osiris's worshippers may not cut down a fruit tree or block a spring.",
  "greek.plut.36":
    "Plutarch reports that the Egyptians call not only the Nile but every kind of moisture 'the efflux of Osiris': a water jar leads his processions, and a rush, standing for the king and the south, means the watering of all things and resembles the generative member. At the Pamylia they carry a statue with a triple member, because the god is the source, and every source multiplies what flows from it, as 'thrice happy' means 'many times'; or, he suggests, 'triple' is meant strictly, since moisture, the origin of all, produced from itself the three first substances, earth, air and fire. The tale that Typhon threw Osiris's member into the river and that Isis made and honoured a replica means, he says, that the god's creative power took moisture as its first substance. He reports another Egyptian tale, that Apopis, brother of the Sun, made war on Zeus, and that Zeus adopted Osiris, who helped him, and named him Dionysus; and he explains it as nature: the Egyptians call the wind 'Zeus', the dry and fiery is its enemy, and moisture feeds the exhalations that strengthen the wind.",
  "greek.plut.37":
    "Plutarch reports that the Greeks consecrate the ivy to Dionysus and that the Egyptian name for ivy is said to be chenosiris, 'plant of Osiris'. He reports that Ariston, writing on Athenian colonies, found a letter of Alexarchus saying that Dionysus was the son of Zeus and Isis and is called not Osiris but Arsaphes, meaning virility, and that Hermaeus, in the first book of The Egyptians, says Osiris means 'sturdy'. He sets aside Mnaseas's linking of Dionysus, Osiris and Serapis to Epaphus, and Anticleides' claim that Isis was Prometheus's daughter and Dionysus's wife. In his own voice he says the festivals and sacrifices already described are more convincing than any authority.",
  "greek.plut.38":
    "Plutarch reports the Egyptians' natural reading. The Dog-star is Isis's, as the bringer of water; they honour the Lion and put gaping lions' heads on temple doors because the Nile rises when the sun enters Leo. As the Nile is the efflux of Osiris, the earth is the body of Isis, not all of it but the part the Nile covers and unites with; Horus is born of that union, and Horus is the seasonable tempering of the air, reared by Leto in the marshes round Buto. The outermost land, by the mountains and the sea, they call Nephthys, 'Finality', the wife of Typhon; when an exceptional flood reaches it, that is Osiris's union with Nephthys, proved by the plants that spring up, among them the melilot whose withering, the story goes, showed Typhon the wrong done to him. So Isis bore Horus lawfully, Nephthys Anubis in secret. The king-lists say Nephthys was at first barren after marrying Typhon; said of the goddess, he explains, that must mean the barrenness of hard-baked land.",
  "greek.plut.39":
    "Plutarch reports the reading of the myth as the seasons of the Nile. Typhon's plot and usurpation is the power of drought, which dries up the moisture that feeds the Nile's rise; his ally the Ethiopian queen is the south wind from Ethiopia, which, when it beats the north (Etesian) winds that drive the rain clouds south, stops the rains, so that the Nile shrinks to a trickle. The shutting of Osiris in the chest means the vanishing of water, which is why they say he disappeared in Athyr, when the Etesian winds have stopped, the Nile is low, the land bare and the nights long. Then, in mournful rites lasting four days from the seventeenth, the priests drape a gilded cow, image of Isis and the earth, in black linen, mourning four things: the Nile's retreat, the death of the north winds, the day growing shorter than the night, and the stripping of earth and trees. On the nineteenth, at night, they go down to the sea, pour fresh water into a small golden coffer in the sacred chest, and shout for joy that Osiris is found; then they knead fertile soil with the water, spices and costly incense into a crescent-shaped figure, which they dress and adorn, showing that they take these gods to be the substance of earth and water.",
  "greek.plut.40":
    "Plutarch reports that when Isis had recovered Osiris and was watching Horus grow strong on mists and clouds, Typhon was beaten but not destroyed: the goddess of the earth would not let the nature opposed to moisture be wiped out, only tempered, since a complete world cannot exist without the fiery element. He reports too the account that Typhon once ruled Osiris's land, because Egypt was once sea, as shells in its mines and hills and the salty springs show; Horus's victory over Typhon was a timely abundance of rain, with which the Nile drove back the sea and built up the fertile land with silt. In his own voice he adds that this can be seen today, as the river advances the land, and that Pharos, a day's sail from Egypt in Homer, is now joined to it. All this, he says, is rather like the Stoics' teaching about the gods, who call the creative, fostering spirit Dionysus, the violent and destructive Heracles, the receptive Ammon, what pervades the earth and its fruits Demeter and the Daughter, and what pervades the sea Poseidon.",
  "greek.plut.41":
    "Plutarch reports that some Egyptians, adding astronomy to these natural explanations, take Typhon to be the world of the sun and Osiris the world of the moon. The moon's light, they reason, is generative and moist, kind to young animals and growing plants, while the sun's untempered heat parches growth, makes much of the earth uninhabitable and often overpowers the moon. That is why the Egyptians call Typhon 'Seth', which they say means 'overmastering and compelling'. They have a story that Heracles lives in the sun and travels round with it, as Hermes does with the moon, the moon's actions being like those of reason and wisdom and the sun's like blows of brute force. He adds that the Stoics say the sun is kindled and fed from the sea, while springs and lakes send the moon a sweet, mild exhalation.",
  "greek.plut.42":
    "Plutarch reports the lunar reading. The Egyptians say Osiris's life ended on the seventeenth of the month, when the full moon is visibly over; the Pythagoreans call that day 'the Barrier' and loathe the number seventeen, because it falls between sixteen and eighteen, the only figures whose perimeters equal their areas, and splits their ratio unequally. Osiris lived, or reigned, twenty-eight years, the number of the moon's days of light. The wood cut at the 'burials of Osiris' is shaped into a crescent coffer, because the moon near the sun becomes a crescent and vanishes; the fourteen pieces of Osiris are the days of the waning moon from full to new; and the day the new moon first appears is called 'Incomplete Good', for Osiris is beneficent. His name, they say, means many things, above all an active and beneficent power, and Hermaeus says his other name, Omphis, means 'benefactor'.",
  "greek.plut.43":
    "Plutarch reports that the Egyptians link the Nile's rise to the moon's phases: the highest rise, at Elephantine, is twenty-eight cubits, the number of the moon's days of light; the lowest, near Mendes and Xoïs, six cubits, its first quarter; the normal rise at Memphis fourteen cubits, the full moon. The Apis, they say, is the living image of Osiris, conceived when a fertile light from the moon strikes a cow in season, which is why its markings, light darkened by shadow, resemble the moon. At the new moon of Phamenoth, the start of spring, they keep a feast called 'Osiris's coming to the Moon'. So they set Osiris's power in the moon and join Isis, as generation, to him, calling the moon the mother of the world, both male and female, made pregnant by the sun and sowing generative principles into the air. Typhon's destructive power, they believe, does not always win, but is often bound by generation and later released to fight Horus, who is the earthly world, never wholly free of either dissolution or birth.",
  "greek.plut.44":
    "Plutarch reports that some read the story as eclipses: the moon is eclipsed only at the full, falling into the earth's shadow as Osiris fell into his coffin; and the moon in turn hides the sun, on the thirtieth of the month, but never destroys it, as Isis did not destroy Typhon. Some call the earth's shadow itself Typhon. He reports that when Nephthys bore Anubis, Isis treated him as her own, because Nephthys is what is below the earth and unseen, Isis what is above and seen, and the horizon that touches both is Anubis, pictured as a dog because the dog sees by night and day; Anubis is to the Egyptians what Hecate is to the Greeks, a god of the underworld and of Olympus. Some take Anubis to be Cronus, who brings forth and conceives all things in himself, and so is called 'Dog'. He reports too that the dog once had the highest honours in Egypt, but lost them when, after Cambyses killed the Apis and threw out its body, the dog alone came near and ate of it.",
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 45–80: two principles, and Plutarch's own reading
  "greek.plut.45":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, concludes that no single explanation is right but all of them together are: Typhon is not drought, wind, sea or darkness alone but everything harmful and destructive in nature. The first principles of the universe are not lifeless atoms, as Democritus and Epicurus say, nor is there, as the Stoics hold, one Reason and one Providence ruling formless matter; for if God caused everything, nothing bad could arise, and if he caused nothing, nothing good. He cites Heracleitus, that the world's harmony is like that of a lyre or bow, springing back when strained, and Euripides, that good and bad cannot be kept apart but are mixed. So, he says, a very ancient belief, of no known source but firmly held among Greeks and barbarians in stories, rites and sacrifices, holds that the universe is not adrift without mind, nor steered by a single Reason, nor dealt good and bad by one keeper of two jars, as Homer's Zeus is, but is shaped by two opposed principles and forces, one leading straight to the right, the other turning us aside; and if not the whole universe, at least this earthly world with its moon is uneven and changeable. Since nothing comes to be without a cause, and the good cannot cause evil, nature must hold a source of evil as well as of good.",
  "greek.plut.46":
    "Plutarch reports that most people, and the wisest, hold there are two rival gods, the maker of good and the maker of evil; some call the better one a god and the other a daemon, as Zoroaster the sage did, who, they record, lived five thousand years before the Trojan War. Zoroaster called them Oromazes and Areimanius, the one most like light and the other like darkness and ignorance, with Mithras between them, whom the Persians call 'Mediator'. He taught offerings of thanks to Oromazes and averting, mournful offerings to Areimanius: they pound a plant called omomi in a mortar, calling on Hades and Darkness, mix it with the blood of a sacrificed wolf, and throw it where the sun never shines. They assign some plants and animals to the good god and others to the evil one: dogs, birds and hedgehogs to the good, water-rats to the evil, so that whoever kills the most water-rats is counted fortunate.",
  "greek.plut.47":
    "Plutarch reports a story the Persians tell, which he calls fabulous. Oromazes, born of the purest light, and Areimanius, born of darkness, are always at war. Oromazes made six gods, of Good Thought, Truth, Order, Wisdom, Wealth, and Pleasure in what is Honourable, and Areimanius made as many rivals. Oromazes tripled his size, moved as far from the sun as the sun is from the earth, adorned the sky with stars and set the Dog-star as their watchman; he made twenty-four more gods and put them in an egg, but Areimanius's creatures pierced it and got in, and so evil is now mixed with good. At a fated time Areimanius, bringing plague and famine, will be destroyed by them; the earth will become level, and one blessed people will have one way of life, one government and one language. He reports Theopompus's version: according to the sages, each god rules in turn for three thousand years, they fight for another three thousand, each undoing the other's works, and in the end Hades passes away, people are happy, need no food and cast no shadow, and the god who brought it about rests for a while, as long as a moderate sleep for a man. Such, he says, is the mythology of the sages.",
  "greek.plut.48":
    "Plutarch reports that the Chaldeans call the planets guardian gods, two of them beneficent, two harmful and three in between, and that the Greeks give the good to Olympian Zeus and the hateful to Hades, and tell that Concord (Harmonia) was born of Aphrodite and Ares, the one gentle and the other harsh. The philosophers, he says, agree. Heracleitus calls War 'the Father and King and Lord of all', blames Homer for praying that strife vanish, since all things come from strife, and says the Sun will not overstep his bounds or the Furies will find him out. Empedocles calls the good principle 'friendship' and Concord 'sedate of countenance', and the bad 'accursed quarrelling' and 'blood-stained strife'. The Pythagoreans list the good as Unity, the Limited, the Resting, the Straight, the Odd, the Square, the Equal, the Right-handed and the Bright, and the bad as their opposites. Anaxagoras sets Mind against the Infinite, Aristotle Form against Privation, and Plato, often veiling it, Identity against Difference; but in the Laws, when older, he says plainly that the universe is moved by not less than two souls, one good and one its opponent, with a third nature between them that depends on both and always longs for the better. In his own voice Plutarch says the rest of the treatise will try to bring the Egyptians' religion into line with this philosophy.",
  "greek.plut.49":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, says the world is made of opposing powers, not equal, the better being stronger; but the bad cannot be wiped out, being inborn in the body and the soul of the universe and always fighting the better. In the soul, mind and reason, ruler of all that is good, is Osiris; in earth, wind, water, the heavens and the stars, whatever is ordered, settled and healthy, as the seasons, temperatures and cycles show, is Osiris's efflux and reflected image. Typhon is the soul's passionate, impulsive, irrational and violent part, and in the body whatever is perishable, sick and disordered, as unseasonable weather and the darkening of sun and moon show. He reports that the Egyptian name Seth means this, 'overmastering', 'overpowering', and often 'turning back' or 'overpassing'; that some say Bebon was one of Typhon's companions, though Manetho says Bebon was another name of Typhon; and that it means 'restraint' or 'hindrance', as Typhon's power checks things moving well towards their end.",
  "greek.plut.50":
    "Plutarch reports that the Egyptians therefore give Typhon the stupidest of tame animals, the ass, and the most savage of wild ones, the crocodile and the hippopotamus. At Hermopolis they show a statue of Typhon as a hippopotamus with a hawk on its back fighting a serpent: the hippopotamus is Typhon, the hawk the power and rule he tries to seize by force and often fails to win, confused by his own wickedness. So on the seventh of Tybi, the feast they call 'the Coming of Isis from Phoenicia', they stamp a bound hippopotamus on their sacred cakes. At Apollonopolis everyone must eat crocodile; on one day they hunt as many as they can and throw the dead ones down before the temple, saying that Typhon escaped Horus by turning into a crocodile. And they count every harmful animal, plant and event as the deed, part or movement of Typhon.",
  "greek.plut.51":
    "Plutarch reports that the Egyptians show Osiris as an eye and a sceptre, forethought and power, much as Homer calls Zeus 'supreme and counsellor'; and often as a hawk, for its keen sight, swift flight and little need of food. The hawk is said to scatter dust on the eyes of the unburied dead, and to raise a feather when it drinks at the river and lower it after, showing it has escaped the crocodile. Everywhere they show statues of Osiris in human form with an erect member, for his creative and nourishing power, and dress them in flame-coloured robes, taking the sun's body to be the visible form of the power for good. In his own voice Plutarch says those who give the sun's orb to Typhon deserve contempt, since Typhon has nothing bright, preserving, ordered or reasonable; drought that kills animals and plants is not the sun's doing but comes when the disorderly power gets loose and the winds and waters are not tempered in season.",
  "greek.plut.52":
    "Plutarch reports rites that link Osiris and Horus with the sun. In the sacred hymns of Osiris they call on 'him who is hidden in the arms of the Sun'; on the thirtieth of Epiphi they keep the birthday of the Eyes of Horus, when sun and moon are in line, treating both as Horus's eye and light. On the eighth of the waning of Phaophi, after the autumn equinox, they keep the birthday of the Staff of the Sun, showing that the sun then needs support as it weakens. At the winter solstice they lead the cow seven times round the Sun's temple, a procession called the Seeking for Osiris, because the goddess longs for water in winter, seven times because the sun passes from winter to summer solstice in the seventh month. Horus son of Isis, it is said, first sacrificed to the Sun on the fourth of the month, as the records called the Birthdays of Horus say. Daily they burn incense to the Sun three times, resin at sunrise, myrrh at midday and kyphi at sunset. He reports that some say outright that Osiris is the Sun and is called Sirius by the Greeks, and some that Isis is the Moon, her horned statues imitating the crescent and her dark robes its hidings as she pursues the Sun; so lovers pray to the Moon, and Eudoxus says Isis presides over love. In his own voice he grants these views some plausibility, but says no one should listen to those who make Typhon the Sun.",
  "greek.plut.53":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, says that Isis is the female principle of nature, receptive of every kind of generation, which is why Plato calls her 'the gentle nurse' and 'the all-receptive', and why most people give her countless names: guided by reason, she turns to this or that and takes on every shape and form. She has an inborn love for the first and most sovereign of all things, which is the same as the good; she longs for it and pursues it, and avoids and rejects the part that comes from evil. She is the place and matter for both, but always leans to the better and offers herself to it, to create from her and fill her with effluxes and likenesses, and she rejoices to be pregnant with them. For creation is the image of being in matter, and what comes to be is a picture of what is.",
  "greek.plut.54":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, reads the myth philosophically. It fits, he says, that they tell how Osiris's soul is everlasting and imperishable while Typhon often tears apart his body and makes it vanish, and Isis wanders in search of it and puts it together again: what truly is, the intelligible and good, is above destruction and change, but the images it stamps on the bodily world, like seals in wax, do not last; disorder drives down on them and fights Horus, whom Isis bears as the visible image of the perceptible world. So Horus is accused by Typhon of being illegitimate, not pure like his father, reason unmixed, but tainted by body; and he wins when Hermes, that is Reason, testifies that nature, changing its form towards the perceptible, rightly brings the world into being. The birth of Apollo from Isis and Osiris while still in Rhea's womb means that before this world was made visible and shaped by reason, nature brought forth a first, imperfect creation; that is why the elder Horus is said to have been born in darkness and lame, a mere sketch of a world to be.",
  "greek.plut.55":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, goes on: this Horus is perfect and complete, but he has not destroyed Typhon, only taken away his activity and strength. He reports that at Kopto the statue of Horus holds Typhon's genitals in one hand, and that Hermes is said to have cut out Typhon's sinews for lyre strings, which, he explains, teaches that Reason tunes the universe and makes harmony out of discord, crippling the destructive power rather than destroying it. That power, weak and inactive here, attaches itself to what is changeable and causes earthquakes, droughts, storms, lightning and thunderbolts, taints waters and winds with plague, and runs wild as far as the moon, darkening it. He reports the Egyptian account that Typhon sometimes strikes the eye of Horus and sometimes snatches it out and swallows it and then gives it back to the Sun, and explains the striking as the moon's monthly waning and the maiming as its eclipse, which the sun heals by shining on it once it leaves the earth's shadow.",
  "greek.plut.56":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, says the better and more divine nature has three parts: the intelligible, matter, and what comes from both, which the Greeks call the world. Plato calls the intelligible the idea, model or father, matter the mother, nurse, seat and place of generation, and what comes of both the offspring. He guesses that the Egyptians most honour the most beautiful triangle, which Plato in the Republic seems to use for his marriage figure: upright three, base four, hypotenuse five, equal in power to the other two. The upright is like the male, the base the female, the hypotenuse their child; so Osiris is the origin, Isis the receiver and Horus the result. He plays on the numbers (three the first perfect odd number, four a square on two, five like both its parents; panta, 'all', from pente, 'five'; five squared the number of the Egyptian letters and of the Apis's years). He reports that the Egyptians call Horus also Min, 'seen', since the world is visible, and Isis Muth, 'mother', Athyri, 'the world-house of Horus', the place and receptacle of generation, as Plato has it, and Methyer, from 'full' and 'cause'.",
  "greek.plut.57":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, suggests that Hesiod, making Chaos, Earth, Tartarus and Love the first of all things, seems to accept the same origins if we call Isis Earth, Osiris Love and Typhon Tartarus, with Chaos beneath as the resting place of the universe. This recalls, he says, Plato's myth in the Symposium, where Socrates tells how Poverty, wanting a child, lay beside Plenty while he slept and bore Love, of a mixed and changeable nature, son of a good, wise and self-sufficient father and a needy mother always clinging to another. Plenty, he explains, is the first beloved and desired, perfect and self-sufficient; Poverty is Plato's name for matter, lacking the good in herself but filled by him and always longing for him. The world, or Horus, born of them, is not eternal or imperishable, but by being always reborn stays young and is never destroyed in the changes and cycles of events.",
  "greek.plut.58":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, says legend must not be treated as history; we should take from each story what fits it by likeness. When we speak of matter we must not follow philosophers who think of a lifeless, formless body inert in itself: oil is the matter of perfume and gold of a statue, and neither lacks qualities; the soul and thought of a man are the matter of understanding and virtue, which reason adorns and tunes, and some have called the mind a place where forms gather and concepts are stamped. Some, he notes, think a woman's seed is not a power or origin but only matter and food for generation. Holding to this, we should think of the goddess too, who always shares with the first god and loves the fair things about him, not as his opponent but as a just man is said to love and a good wife to long for her husband: always clinging to him, pressing on him and filled with the purest and highest principles.",
  "greek.plut.59":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, continues: where Typhon forces his way in and seizes the outermost parts, Isis seems sad and is said to mourn, seeking the remains and scattered limbs of Osiris and arranging them, receiving and hiding what perishes and bringing forth again what is born. The god's forms, relations and effluxes abide in the heavens and the stars; but what is spread among things that can be acted on, earth, sea, plants and animals, is dissolved, destroyed and buried, and often shines forth again in new births. That is why, he says, the story makes Typhon the husband of Nephthys and Osiris her secret lover: the destructive power rules most over the outermost part of matter, called Nephthys or Finality, while the creative power gives it only a weak seed, which Typhon destroys except for what Isis takes up, keeps, nourishes and makes strong.",
  "greek.plut.60":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, says that, as Plato and Aristotle think, this god is the better; what creates and preserves in nature moves towards him and towards being, what destroys moves away towards not-being. So, he says, the name Isis comes from Greek words for hastening with understanding and being carried on, since she is living and intelligent motion; it is not a foreign name, just as all gods share a name from 'visible' and 'running', and from her understanding and movement we and the Egyptians alike call her Isis. He adds that Plato says the ancients called 'essence' (ousia) 'isia', and spoke of understanding as a movement of mind hastening on, giving good names to what flows and moves and bad names to what binds and checks nature's going: 'ill-going' for badness, 'difficulty of going' for helplessness, 'fear of going' for cowardice, 'not going' for distress.",
  "greek.plut.61":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, derives the name Osiris from the Greek 'holy' and 'sacred', since he is the joint relation of things in heaven, once called sacred, and in the world below, once called holy. The relation that reveals heavenly things and belongs to what tends upward is called Anubis, or Hermanubis, as belonging partly above and partly below; so, he reports, they sacrifice to him a white cock and a saffron-coloured one. He says there is nothing surprising in finding Greek forms in these names: many Greek words went abroad with emigrants and live on as strangers in foreign lands. He reports that the so-called books of Hermes say the power that guides the sun's course is called Horus by the Egyptians and Apollo by the Greeks, that the power over the wind is called by some Osiris and by others Serapis, and that Sothis means 'pregnancy', whence the Greek name of the Dog-star, Isis's star. He says one need not be eager about such names, but he would rather concede Serapis to the Egyptians as foreign, and keep Osiris as Greek, both names of one god and one power.",
  "greek.plut.62":
    "Plutarch reports that Egyptian beliefs are like these. They often call Isis Athena, meaning, he says, 'I came of myself', self-moved motion; Typhon, as said, they call Seth, Bebon and Smu, names meaning a forcible check, opposition or reversal. They call the lodestone the bone of Horus and iron the bone of Typhon, as Manetho records; in his own voice he explains that as iron is sometimes drawn to the stone and sometimes repelled, so the good and rational movement of the world sometimes draws and calms the harsh Typhonian movement by persuasion and sometimes gathers itself and drives it back into difficulty. He reports Eudoxus's account of an Egyptian myth that Zeus, his legs grown together, could not walk and stayed in the wilderness for shame, until Isis cut and separated them and so let him move swiftly; the fable, he says, teaches that the god's mind and reason, fixed in the unseen, went forth into generation by reason of motion.",
  "greek.plut.63":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, explains the sistrum, the rattle used in the rites. It shows that everything that exists must be shaken and never stop moving, roused when it grows sleepy; they say they drive off Typhon with sistrums, meaning that when destruction binds nature, generation frees and wakes it by motion. The rounded top encloses four rattling bars, because the part of the world that is born and dies lies under the moon's orb and changes through the four elements, fire, earth, water and air. At the top is a cat with a human face, and below the bars the faces of Isis on one side and Nephthys on the other, standing for birth and death. The cat stands for the moon, for its varied colouring, night activity and fertility: it is said to bear one, then two, and so on up to seven kittens, twenty-eight in all, the number of the moon's days of light, which he allows may sound like a fable; but its pupils, he says, grow round at full moon and thin as it wanes, and its human face shows the mind and reason that guide the moon's changes.",
  "greek.plut.64":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, sums up: it is not right to believe that water, sun, earth or sky is Osiris or Isis, or that fire, drought or the sea is Typhon. We shall not go wrong if we give to Typhon whatever in these is excessive, deficient and disordered, and revere whatever is orderly, good and helpful as the work of Isis and the image, reflection and reason of Osiris. That also answers Eudoxus's puzzles about why Isis, not Demeter, presides over love, and why Dionysus cannot raise the Nile or rule the dead: by one argument these gods are set over every part of the good, and all that is fair and good in nature exists through them, Osiris giving the origins and Isis receiving and distributing them.",
  "greek.plut.65":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, turns to the many tiresome people who tie theology to the changes of the weather, the growth of crops, sowing and ploughing, saying that Osiris is buried when the grain is sown and covered, and comes back to life when the shoots appear. He reports what they tell in support: that Isis, finding herself pregnant, put on an amulet on the sixth of Phaophi, and about the winter solstice bore Harpocrates, imperfect and premature, among the first flowers and shoots, so that they offer him the first-fruits of lentils and keep his birthdays after the spring equinox. People who hear this, he says, are content and believe it, drawing a plausible explanation straight from what is familiar.",
  "greek.plut.66":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, says there is no harm in such explanations on two conditions. First, they must keep the gods common to all peoples, not make them Egyptian only, nor confine their names to the Nile and the land it waters, nor deny the great gods to people without a Nile, a Buto or a Memphis; all peoples know Isis and her fellow gods, even if they learned their Egyptian names only lately, and have always honoured their powers. Second, and more important, they must take care not to dissolve the divine into winds, streams, sowings, ploughings and seasons, as those do who call wine Dionysus and flame Hephaestus; he reports Cleanthes calling the breath borne through the crops and destroyed Phersephonê, and a poet speaking of reapers cutting the limbs of Demeter. Such people, he says, are like those who take sails and anchor for the pilot, or warp and woof for the weaver, and they plant atheistic opinions by giving gods' names to senseless, lifeless things that men must destroy in using them.",
  "greek.plut.67":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, says that God is not senseless, lifeless or under human control; we count as gods those who give us these things and supply us with what is lasting. Nor are there different gods for different peoples, barbarian and Greek, southern and northern: as sun, moon, sky, earth and sea are common to all though named differently, so for the one reason that orders all things, the one Providence that watches over them, and the powers set under it, different peoples have different honours and names according to their customs. Men use consecrated symbols, some obscure, some clearer, to lead the mind to divine things, but not without danger: some go astray into superstition, and others, fleeing superstition as from a bog, fall unawares over a cliff into atheism.",
  "greek.plut.68":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, says that in these mysteries philosophy's reasoning must be our guide, and we must think reverently about everything said and done, so as not to take the laws' fine rules about sacrifices and feasts in the wrong spirit, as Theodorus complained his hearers took with the left hand what he offered with the right. He reports Egyptian customs that show everything should be referred to reason: at the feast of Hermes on the nineteenth of the first month they eat honey and a fig, saying 'A sweet thing is Truth'; Isis's amulet, said to hang at her neck, is interpreted 'a true voice'; in Mesore they offer Harpocrates legumes, saying 'The tongue is luck, the tongue is god'; and the persea is sacred to the goddess because its fruit is like a heart and its leaf like a tongue. Harpocrates, he says, is not an imperfect infant god or a guardian of beans but the corrector of men's half-formed, unreasoned talk about the gods, which is why he holds a finger to his lips. Nothing a man has is more divine than reasoning about the gods; so, he says, those who come down to the oracle here at Delphi are told to think holy thoughts and speak words of good omen, while most people in processions proclaim good words and then say and think the most unholy things.",
  "greek.plut.69":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, asks how to deal with gloomy, mournful sacrifices, when it is not right either to drop the customary rites or to confuse our beliefs about the gods. He reports that the Greeks do much the same at about the same time: at Athens women fast sitting on the ground at the Thesmophoria, and the Boeotians move the halls of the Goddess of Sorrow and call the feast the Festival of Sorrow, because Demeter grieves for her Daughter's descent to Pluto. This sowing month, at the setting of the Pleiades, is Athyr to the Egyptians, Pyanepsion to the Athenians and Damatrius to the Boeotians. He reports Theopompus's account that the western peoples call winter Cronus, summer Aphrodite and spring Persephone, and derive all things from Cronus and Aphrodite; that the Phrygians think the god sleeps in winter and wakes in summer, singing him lullabies and then waking-songs like bacchants; and that the Paphlagonians say he is bound in winter and frees himself in spring.",
  "greek.plut.70":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, suggests the gloom comes from the season, when the crops and fruits vanish, which the ancients did not take for gods but for the gods' necessary gifts that saved them from a savage life. Seeing fruits disappear from the trees while they sowed others meanly, scraping the earth with their hands and burying seed with uncertain hope, they acted like mourners at a funeral. And as we say someone buying Plato's books 'buys Plato', they called the gods' gifts by the gods' names in gratitude. Later people took this blindly and, in ignorance, called the comings and goings of crops the births and deaths of gods, and even believed it, filling their minds with absurd and confused notions. Rightly, he says, Xenophanes of Colophon told the Egyptians that if they thought these were gods they should not mourn them, and if they mourned them they should not think them gods; and it is ridiculous to pray amid lamentations that the powers make the crops grow again, only to be eaten and mourned once more.",
  "greek.plut.71":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, corrects himself: that is not quite what happens; people mourn their crops and pray to the gods, the makers and givers, to make new crops grow in place of those being destroyed. He cites the philosophers' saying that those who have not learned to use words rightly go wrong in their actions too: some Greeks call bronze, painted and stone images 'gods' rather than statues and offerings to the gods, and then dare to say that Lachares stripped Athena, that Dionysius sheared Apollo's golden locks, and that Capitoline Jupiter was burned in the civil war. The Egyptians, he says, have suffered this most over their sacred animals. The Greeks rightly say the dove is sacred to Aphrodite, the serpent to Athena, the raven to Apollo and the dog to Artemis; but most Egyptians serve the animals themselves as gods, making their rites ridiculous and, worse, breeding a dangerous belief that sinks the weak into superstition and drives the bold into atheistic, brutish reasoning. So he will set out what seem to be the facts.",
  "greek.plut.72":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, rejects two explanations of the animal cults: that the gods, fearing Typhon, hid themselves in the bodies of ibises, dogs and hawks, a fancy beyond all monstrous fable; and that the surviving souls of the dead are reborn only into these animals. He reports political explanations: that Osiris, on his great expedition, divided his army into companies and gave each an animal standard, which its descendants held sacred; that later kings wore gold and silver masks of beasts' heads in battle to terrify enemies; and that a crafty king, seeing the Egyptians fickle but unbeatable when united, set different peoples to worship different animals, which are enemies by nature, so that the peoples would quarrel forever defending their own. He reports that the people of Lycopolis alone eat sheep, because the wolf they worship does; and that in his own day the people of Oxyrhynchus caught, sacrificed and ate a dog because the people of Cynopolis were eating the oxyrhynchus fish, so that they went to war and did each other much harm until the Romans punished both.",
  "greek.plut.73":
    "Plutarch reports that many say Typhon's own soul was divided among these animals, which would mean that all irrational and brutish nature belongs to the evil god, and that they tend the animals to soothe him. In a great drought bringing plague or other disasters, the priests secretly lead off some of the sacred animals at night, first threatening and frightening them and, if the drought goes on, consecrating and sacrificing them, as though punishing the god or performing a great purification. He reports that in the city of Eileithyia, as Manetho records, they used to burn men alive, calling them Typhonians, and scatter their ashes with winnowing fans, publicly, in the dog-days; but the sacrifices of the sacred animals were secret and at no fixed time, unknown to the people except at the animals' burials, when they display some other sacred animals and throw them into the grave together, thinking to vex Typhon and curb his pleasure. The Apis and a few others seem sacred to Osiris, most animals to Typhon. In his own voice he says that, if this is true, the inquiry concerns the animals honoured by all: the ibis, the hawk, the cynocephalus, the Apis, and the goat of Mendes.",
  "greek.plut.74":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, says that what remains is the animals' usefulness and their symbolism, some sharing in one and many in both. The Egyptians clearly honour the cow, the sheep and the ichneumon for their usefulness, as the people of Lemnos honour larks for destroying locusts' eggs and the Thessalians storks for killing snakes, with exile for anyone who kills one. They honoured the asp, the weasel and the beetle because they saw in them faint likenesses of the gods' power, like images of the sun in drops of water. He reports beliefs about them: that the weasel conceives through the ear and gives birth through the mouth, a likeness of the birth of speech; that beetles have no females and roll their seed in a ball by pushing it backwards, as the sun seems to turn the heavens against its own course from west to east; and that the asp is like lightning, never growing old and moving easily without limbs.",
  "greek.plut.75":
    "Plutarch reports that the crocodile is honoured, not without plausible reason, as a living image of God, because it is the only creature without a tongue, and the divine Word needs no voice; because a transparent membrane lets it see without being seen, as the First God does; because the female lays her eggs exactly where the coming flood will stop, keeping them dry; and because it lays sixty eggs, hatches them in sixty days and lives at most sixty years, sixty being the astronomers' first measure. Of animals honoured for both use and symbol: the ibis kills deadly reptiles, taught men purging by dosing herself, drinks only clean water (so the strictest priests take their purifying water where she has drunk), makes an equilateral triangle with her feet and bill, and has black and white plumage like the moon's first quarter. In his own voice he says the Egyptians' delight in slight likenesses is no stranger than the Greeks': a Cretan Zeus without ears, Pheidias's serpent beside Athena and the tortoise beside Aphrodite at Elis, Poseidon's trident for the third region; and he reports the Pythagoreans' naming of numbers and figures after gods, the triangle Athena, one Apollo, two Strife, three Justice, and thirty-six, the 'World', their greatest oath.",
  "greek.plut.76":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, says that if the greatest philosophers found the riddle of the divine even in lifeless and bodiless things and would treat nothing carelessly, all the more should we welcome the special properties of creatures that perceive and have soul, feeling and character: not to honour them, but to honour the divine through them, as clearer mirrors of it and instruments of the god who orders all things. Nothing lifeless is better than what lives, nothing without perception better than what perceives, not even all the gold and emeralds in the world; the divine is not born in colours, shapes or polished surfaces, and lifeless things have less honour than the dead. A nature that lives, sees, moves itself and knows what is its own and what is another's has drawn an efflux of beauty from the Intelligence 'by which the Universe is guided', as Heracleitus says. So the divine is no worse shown in these animals than in bronze and stone, which perish and perceive nothing. This, he says, is what he most approves of in the accounts of the sacred animals.",
  "greek.plut.77":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, explains the robes. Isis's robes are many-coloured, because her power is over matter, which becomes and receives everything, light and darkness, day and night, fire and water, life and death, beginning and end. Osiris's robe has no shading or variety but one colour, like light, because the first principle is unmixed and purely intelligible; so once it has been taken off it is put away unseen and untouched, while Isis's robes are used again and again, since things of the senses can be seen many times as they change. But the understanding of the intelligible, pure and simple, flashes through the soul like lightning and can be touched and seen only once. That is why, he says, Plato and Aristotle call this part of philosophy the 'epoptic' or mystic part: those who have passed beyond the confused matters of opinion leap by reason to that first, simple, immaterial principle, and when they have somehow touched the pure truth about it, they think they hold the whole of philosophy.",
  "greek.plut.78":
    "Plutarch, in his own voice, gives what he takes to be the priests' guarded secret: that Osiris is ruler and king of the dead, none other than the god the Greeks call Hades and Pluto. Because people do not understand how this is so, it troubles them, as if the holy Osiris dwelt in and under the earth where the bodies of the dead are hidden. In fact, he says, Osiris is far from the earth, untouched and pure of all matter subject to death; souls in this life, bound up with bodies and passions, can share in him only through a dim vision gained by philosophy. But when they are freed and pass to the unseen, pure realm beyond passion, this god becomes their leader and king, and they depend on him, gazing without end and longing for the beauty that cannot be spoken. With that beauty, as the old story says, Isis is forever in love, pursuing it, joining with it, and filling our world with all the fair and good things that share in coming to be. This, he says, is the account most fitting for the gods.",
  "greek.plut.79":
    "Plutarch turns, as he promised, to the daily incense, and says in his own voice that the Egyptians set health beside piety in their rites and rules, thinking it wrong to serve what is pure with sick bodies or souls. The air changes: at night it grows thick, weighs on the body and depresses the soul, so, he reports, on rising they burn resin, whose strong scent purifies the air and revives the spirit in the body. At midday, when the sun draws a heavy exhalation from the earth into the air, they burn myrrh, whose heat breaks up the murky, swollen masses in the air. He adds that physicians relieve plagues with large fires that thin the air, better still with fragrant woods such as cypress, juniper and pine; that Acron won fame at Athens in the great plague by lighting fires beside the sick; and that Aristotle says sweet smells from perfumes, flowers and meadows help health as much as pleasure, gently relaxing the naturally cold brain. If the Egyptians call myrrh 'bal', meaning 'dissipation of repletion', that supports his explanation.",
  "greek.plut.80":
    "Plutarch reports that kyphi, the incense burned at sunset, is made of sixteen ingredients: honey, wine, raisins, cyperus, resin, myrrh, aspalathus, seselis, mastic, bitumen, rush, sorrel, the greater and lesser junipers, cardamom and calamus, mixed not at random but while the sacred writings are read to the perfumers. In his own voice he notes that sixteen, the square of a square and the only square whose perimeter equals its area, is admirable but adds little here. Its scents, he says, change the air and gently move the body towards sleep, loosening the knots of daily care without wine, and brightening the dream-making faculty like a mirror, as the Pythagoreans' lyre music before sleep calmed the irrational part of the soul. It is used as a drink and a salve, cleansing the inner organs. Resin and myrrh come from the sun's heat drawing them out of trees, while some ingredients of kyphi thrive in night winds, shade, dew and damp; daylight is single and simple, as Pindar says the sun is seen 'through the deserted aether', but the night air is a mixture of many lights and forces, like seeds from every star. So it is fitting, he concludes, to burn resin and myrrh, simple and sun-born, by day, and kyphi, compounded of every kind, at nightfall.",
};
